    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_5675beee45daead4e771a3e499cb407c\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40501988\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nHere, we solve cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/2qscm-gs990\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids | CaltechAUTHORS\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"CaltechAUTHORS\\nCommunities\\nMy dashboard\\nLog in\\nCaltechAUTHORS\\nPublished July 1, 2026 | Version Published\\nJournal Article Open\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nCreators\\n* Brittain, Tyler J. 1\\n* Jang, Seongmin 1 ORCID icon\\n* Coughlin, Gerard M. 1 ORCID icon\\n* Hoang, Jonathan D. 1 ORCID icon\\n* Barcelona, Bre'Anna H. 1\\n* Giriat, Izabela 1\\n* Ristic, Fiona 1\\n* Appling, Nathan 1 ORCID icon\\n* Chossis, Camille PMA 1\\n* Shay, Timothy F. 1 ORCID icon\\n* Gradinaru, Viviana 1 ORCID icon\\nShow affiliations\\n1.\\nROR icon California Institute of Technology\\nAbstract\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to adverse events. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22- and 2.20-Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo .\\nOverall, we provide a framework for using structural information to guide rational engineering of gene delivery vectors to achieve safe and effective delivery.\\nWe thank all Gradinaru lab and CLOVER staff members for their thoughtful assistance. We thank Alex Jin Chung and Yaping Lei for technical support. We thank Catherine Oikonomou for help with manuscript editing. All cryo-EM data were collected at the Caltech cryo-EM facility.\\nContributions\\nTyler J. Brittain and Seongmin Jang contributed equally.\\nS.J. and V.G. conceived and T.J.B., S.J., T.F.S., and V.G. designed the study. T.J.B. performed cryo-EM data collection and processing. T.B. and S.J. analyzed the structure data. S.J. performed and analyzed the BLI experiments. T.J.B., S.J., and N.A. performed molecular cloning. S.J.\\nperformed the confocal imaging. T.J.B. quantified liver de-targeting and brain tropism. S.J. quantified muscle transduction. G.M.C. and S.J. performed ddPCR. T.J.B. and S.J. wrote the manuscript and prepared the figures. V.G. supervised all aspects of the work.\\nCryo-EM maps and atomic models have been deposited with the accession codes EMDB (Electron Microscopy Data Bank): 71273 and PDB: 9P4L (CAP-B10), EMDB: 71278 and PDB: 9P4Q (CAP-B10-PKD2), EMDB: 71277 and PDB: 9P4P (AAV9-X1), EMDB: 71279 and PDB: 9P4R (AAV9-X1.1), EMDB: 71275 and PDB: 9P4N (AAV9-X1.1-PKD2), EMDB: 71274 and PDB: 9P4M (AAV9-B10), and\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.citeab.com/publication/41594783-41906393-structural-basis-of-liver-de-targeting-and-neuronal\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of... - CiteAb\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Antibodies Biochemicals Experimental Models Instruments Kits Nucleotides Proteins\\nSearch\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids.\\nAbstract\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to adverse events. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22- and 2.20-Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo.\\nOverall, we provide a framework for using structural information to guide rational engineering of gene delivery vectors to achieve safe and effective delivery. Copyright © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\\nAuthors\\nTyler J Brittain, Seongmin Jang, Gerard M Coughlin, Jonathan D Hoang, Bre'Anna H Barcelona, Izabela Giriat, Fiona Ristic, Nathan Appling, Camille P M A Chossis, Timothy F Shay, Viviana Gradinaru\\nDivision of Biology & Biological Engineering, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena CA 91125, USA; Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Caltech, Pasadena, CA 91125, USA. Electronic address: viviana@caltech.edu.\\nView Full Text PDF Article Galaxy PDF\\nLoading published images for this product\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1525001626002157\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 7 — Camille P.M.A. Chossis 1 , Timothy F. Shay 1 , Viviana Gradinaru.\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 6 — We thank all Gradinaru lab and CLOVER staff members for their thoughtful assistance. We thank Alex Jin Chung and Yaping Lei for technical\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\ndi TJ Brittain · 2026 · Citato da 8 — We present seven cryo-EM structures of CNS targeting and/or liver de-targeted AAVs, along with their AAVR binding affinities. Based on the\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 6 — Tyler J. Brittain 1 3 we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures. AAVs achieve liver de-targeting, solving seven cryo-EM structures\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nThe present work expands this understanding of the AAVR interface to VR-IV. Previously reported cryo-EM structures (PDB: 7WQO and 7WJX )\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 8 — AAV . CAP - B10 ( CAP - B10 ),24 which more effectively de-targets from the liver in mice. Furthermore, CAP - B10 shows a neuron-specific tropism with\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 8 — engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver. AV.CAP-B10 (CAP-B10),24 which more effectively de-targets from the liver in mice. from The\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 8 — Molecular Therapy Volume 34, Issue 7, 1 July 2026, we present a broad structural study of the molecular mechanism. AAVs achieve liver de-targeting, Rego, I.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Seongmin-Jang-2227246001\",\n      \"title\": \"Seongmin Jang's research works | California Institute of Technology and other places\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Article\\nNovember 2025\\n·\\n9 Reads\\n·\\n4 Citations\\nCell Reports\\nChangfan Lin\\n·\\nXinhong Chen\\n·\\nJonathan D. Hoang\\n·\\n[...]\\n·\\nViviana Gradinaru\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\n* Preprint\\n* File available\\nJune 2025\\n·\\n18 Reads\\n·\\n2 Citations\\nTyler J Brittain\\n·\\nSeongmin Jang\\n·\\nGerard M Coughlin\\n·\\n[...]\\n·\\nViviana Gradinaru\\nCrossing the blood-brain barrier while minimizing liver transduction is a key challenge in developing safe adeno-associated virus (AAV) vectors for treating brain disorders. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solve cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a structural motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirm by measuring affinities.\\nWe show that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1 and AAV9-X1.1, without and with PKD2, at 3.09, 2.51, and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo.\\nOverall, our findings demonstrate that rationally modulating AAVR affinity can alter liver targeting and cellular tropism.\\nRead more\\nDownload\\nAAVs Targeting Human Carbonic Anhydrase IV Enhance Gene Delivery to the Brain\\n* Preprint\\n* File available\\nApril 2025\\n·\\n56 Reads\\nChangfan Lin\\n·\\nXinhong Chen\\n·\\nJonathan D. Hoang\\n·\\n[...]\\n·\\nViviana Gradinaru\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Structure/pdb/9P4Q\",\n      \"title\": \"9P4Q: Cryo-EM structure of AAV.CAP-B10 complexed to AAVR-PKD2\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"9P4Q: Cryo-EM structure of AAV.CAP-B10 complexed to AAVR-PKD2\\nPDB ID: 9P4QDownload. MMDB ID: 280380. PDB Deposition Date: 2025/6/17. Updated in MMDB: 2026/04. Experimental Method: electron microscopy. Resolution: 2.2 Å.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.clover.caltech.edu/news/structural-basis-of-aav-liver-de-targeting-and-neuronal-tropism\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of AAV liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism\\n     - \\n    \\n    CLOVER Center\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-03-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of AAV liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism\\nMarch 30, 2026\\nCLOVER-affiliated graduate student Tyler Brittain and Postdoctoral Fellow Seongmin Jang led a team effort to determine the structural basis of AAV tropism modulation away from liver and non-neuronal CNS cells using cryo-EM, biophysical assays, and in vivo characterization of CAP-B10 , AAV9-X1.1 , and other capsids.\\nCoauthors include current and former CLOVER staff Izabela Giriat, Fiona Ristic, and Tim Shay.\\nCheck out the full story at Molecular Therapy , where it was featured on the cover of the July issue , and discussed in a commentary highlighting the work .\\nCongrats to Tyler, Seongmin, and the team!\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.06.01.729174v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Liver-directed AAV gene therapy metabolically\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.01.729174 ; this version posted June 2, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\\n2\\nCorrespondence should be addressed to:\\n20\\n*Sien Lequeue, Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Liver Therapy and Evolution team, In Vitro Toxicology\\n21\\nand Dermato-Cosmetology (IVTD), Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, 1090 Brussels, Belgium.\\n22\\nE-mail: sien.lequeue@vub.be, Tel: +32 (0)2 477 45 20\\n23\\n**Joery De Kock, Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Liver Therapy and Evolution team, In Vitro Toxicology\\n24\\nand Dermato-Cosmetology (IVTD), Faculty of Medicine and Pharmacy, 1090 Brussels, Belgium.\\n25\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://cryoem.caltech.edu/people/graduate-lab-assistant\",\n      \"title\": \"Tyler J. Brittain - Cryo-EM Facility - Caltech\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Tyler J. Brittain - Cryo-EM Facility - Caltech\\nBrittain Graduate Student Job: tbrittai@caltech.edu Profile: Graduate Research Assistant Caltech California Institute of Technology. Pasadena, California 91125\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://glab.caltech.edu/about\",\n      \"title\": \"People\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"People\\nTyler Brittain, B.S.. GRADUATE STUDENT – BIOCHEMISTRY AND MOLECULAR BIOPHYSICS. Tyler received his B.S. degree in Biophysical Chemistry from James Madison\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea49951a0